283 0 obj
<<
/Title (Pyromaniacs play mostly from the heart, they love to close their eyes, take risks, try difficult fast phrases beyond their technical ability, play complex sounds they may not even understand theoretically. PROS: creative, spontaneous, full of energy, excitement. CONS: often loose the form, make tons of mistakes, impatience.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 239 0 R
/Next 285 0 R
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Title (For the Control-Freaks)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 286 0 R
/Next 240 0 R
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Title (Control-Freaks like organization, they like to play over the grid \(rhythmic accuracy\), respect the form, keep solos under control, addressing the chords properly. They like to understand what they are playing, and have a more pedagogical approach to improvisation. PROS: excellent analytical skills, good team player, can practice with more focus, overtime can grow and improve a lot. CONS: lack of spontaneity and risk taking.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 283 0 R
/Next 287 0 R
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Title (Here are a few exercises I suggest:)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 288 0 R
/Next 284 0 R
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Title (Of course this is a spectrum, so each musician will fall on some point of this scale. Ideally we would all want to be right in the middle.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 285 0 R
/Next 288 0 R
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Title (Once I identify where a student fits in this spectrum, I offer a set of exercises to help encourage the other side. Pyromaniacs need to learn structure, form, rhythmic rigor and discipline. Control-Freaks need to learn how to take risks, be spontaneous and less rational.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 287 0 R
/Next 286 0 R
>>
endobj
